arXiv:2509.16663v1 [stat.ML] 20 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">System-Level Uncertainty Quantification with Multiple Machine Learning Models: A Theoretical Framework</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiaoping Du
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">School of Mechanical Engineering</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Purdue University</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">August 24, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">ML models have errors when used for predictions. The errors are unknown but can be quantified by model uncertainty. When multiple ML models are trained using the same training points, their model uncertainties may be statistically dependent. In reality, model inputs are also random with input uncertainty. The effects of these types of uncertainty must be considered in decision-making and design. This study develops a theoretical framework that generates the joint distribution of multiple ML predictions given the joint distribution of model uncertainties and the joint distribution of model inputs. The strategy is to decouple the coupling between the two types of uncertainty and transform them as independent random variables. The framework lays a foundation for numerical algorithm development for various specific applications.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The increasing adoption of machine learning (ML) models in engineering analysis and design has enhanced decision-making processes by providing rapid predictions for complex systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. However, the inherent nature of ML predictions introduces critical challenges related to uncertainty. This section introduces the background and concepts of uncertainty in ML-based predictions, reviews relevant literature, and outlines the motivation and contributions of this paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Sources of Uncertainty in ML Predictions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In real-world engineering applications, two primary sources of uncertainty affect ML model predictions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Uncertainty (Epistemic Uncertainty):</span> ML models are approximations of underlying physical phenomena or complex simulations. Their predictions are subject to inherent errors due to limited training data, model misspecification, or extrapolation beyond the sampled design space. This uncertainty, often termed model or epistemic uncertainty, reflects the lack of knowledge about the true system behavior. Many advanced ML methods, such as Gaussian Process (GP) regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and Bayesian Neural Networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, are designed to provide not only point predictions but also probabilistic outcomes (e.g., mean and variance) that quantify this epistemic uncertainty.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input Uncertainty (Aleatory Uncertainty):</span> Beyond the model itself, the input parameters to ML models are rarely deterministic in practical scenarios. Material properties, environmental conditions, manufacturing tolerances, and operational loads often exhibit inherent variability. This variability, known as input uncertainty, is aleatory in nature, representing the irreducible randomness.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Coupled Uncertainty and its Consequences</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A significant challenge arises because these two types of uncertainty are often coupled. Specifically, the distribution parameters of model uncertainty (e.g., the predictive mean and variance of a GP model) are functions of the random inputs. This means that the epistemic uncertainty itself varies depending on the aleatory uncertainty in the inputs. The presence of such coupled uncertainty has profound consequences for predictions, decision-making, and design:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unreliable Predictions:</span> Ignoring or improperly handling coupled uncertainty can lead to predictions that are overly confident or inaccurate, potentially compromising the safety and reliability of engineered systems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Suboptimal Decisions:</span> Engineering decisions, such as design optimization or risk assessment, rely on accurate probabilistic information. Coupled uncertainty can obscure the true risk picture, leading to suboptimal or even unsafe design choices.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inefficient Designs:</span> Designs that do not account for the full spectrum of uncertainties may be either over-designed (leading to unnecessary cost) or under-designed (leading to potential failure).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore, it is crucial to accurately estimate the probability distribution of a machine learning prediction by accounting for all sources of uncertainty. Existing approaches to handling coupled uncertainty often focus on predicting marginal distributions of individual model outputs and rely on the assumption that these outputs are independent.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>System Uncertainty Quantification</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Dependent Model Uncertainties and System Uncertainty Quantification</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">System uncertainty quantification (UQ), such as system reliability analysis under input uncertainty, is inherently challenging. The difficulty increases significantly when multiple machine learning (ML) models are involved—particularly when their predictive uncertainties are statistically dependent. This dependence often arises when the models are trained concurrently on shared datasets. For example, in multi-output Gaussian Processes (e.g., Co-Kriging), the outputs of several GP models may follow a joint distribution, indicating statistical dependence among their predictive uncertainties. In such cases, a rigorous approach to system UQ is required to evaluate the combined effects of both input uncertainty and dependent model uncertainties on multiple ML predictions. The complexity of system UQ in this context arises from:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The need to characterize and propagate the joint distribution of input uncertainty.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The need to characterize and propagate the joint distribution of dependent model uncertainty.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The coupling between input and model uncertainties, where distribution parameters of model uncertainty are functions of random inputs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The challenge of combining these dependent and coupled random variables to obtain the joint distribution of multiple ML predictions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Overview of this Study</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study addresses these complexities by developing a theoretical framework for system UQ. The key features are:</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a strategy to explicitly decouple the inherent coupling between input uncertainty and model uncertainty, transforming them into independent random variables. This simplifies the subsequent propagation of uncertainty.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a detailed theoretical framework for generating the joint distribution of multiple ML predictions, given the joint distribution of model uncertainties (including dependent ones) and the joint distribution of model inputs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present fundamental probabilistic transformations, including the conversion of correlated Gaussian variables into independent standard normal variables, which forms a cornerstone of the proposed framework.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This framework lays a robust foundation for the development of numerical algorithms applicable to a wide range of specific engineering applications, enabling more reliable predictions and informed decision-making under complex uncertainty conditions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We treat all uncertainty in an ML model as model uncertainty. It arises from both aleatory sources (such as data noise) and epistemic sources (such as model limitations). For engineering users, these two parts may not be separated. Once a model is trained and delivered, the user cannot reduce the aleatory uncertainty within it or retrain the model to address epistemic uncertainty. Therefore, the total predictive uncertainty is taken as a single source of model uncertainty.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>The Task</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section outlines the theoretical framework for propagating system uncertainty in ML model predictions. The primary task is to determine the joint distribution of multiple ML model outputs, considering both input and model uncertainties, particularly when model uncertainties are statistically dependent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Given:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ML models with input vector <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=(X_{1},\dots,X_{n_{X}})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=(X_{1},\dots,X_{n_{X}})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and output vector <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}=(Y_{1},\dots,Y_{n_{Y}})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}=(Y_{1},\dots,Y_{n_{Y}})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each ML model is defined as <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}=g_{i}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}=g_{i}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\dots,n_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\dots,n_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The joint distribution of model uncertainty for <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is characterized by the conditional joint CDF of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta}_{Y}(\mathbf{X}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta}_{Y}(\mathbf{X}))</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}_{Y}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}_{Y}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the parameters of this joint conditional distribution, which are functions of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The joint distribution of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, characterized by its joint cumulative distribution function (CDF), <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐗</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Find:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The joint distribution of the output vector <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, characterized by its joint CDF, <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This section first considers the scenario with only model uncertainty and then extends the results to include coupled uncertainty.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Case 1: No Input Uncertainty in <math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> (X=x, deterministic)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection considers the scenario where the input vector <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is deterministic, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, there is no input uncertainty, and the focus is solely on quantifying the effects of model uncertainty on ML model prediction at this fixed input <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each ML model output <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the vector <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, its probabilistic response is characterized by a marginal distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{Y_{i}}(Y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{Y_{i}}(Y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. An auxiliary scalar random variable <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is used for the probability transform:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}=F_{Y_{i}}(Y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{x}))\quad\text{for }i=1,\dots,n_{Y}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}=F_{Y_{i}}(Y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{x}))\quad\text{for }i=1,\dots,n_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> contains the distribution parameters. Using the auxiliary scalar random variable <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has been applied in component reliability analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. It is also the fundamental principle used in Monte Carlo simulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By the properties of the probability transform, each <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> follows a uniform distribution on <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}\sim Unif[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}\sim Unif[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>). The vector <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}=(Z_{1},\dots,Z_{n_{Y}})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}=(Z_{1},\dots,Z_{n_{Y}})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the dependence structure among the model uncertainties of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. The original ML model output vector <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> can then be recovered explicitly by inverting this transformation for each component:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}=F_{Y_{i}}^{-1}(Z_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{x}))\quad\text{for }i=1,\dots,n_{Y}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}=F_{Y_{i}}^{-1}(Z_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{x}))\quad\text{for }i=1,\dots,n_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In vector form, this can be written as:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{F}_{\mathbf{Y}}^{-1}(\mathbf{Z};\bm{\theta}_{Y}(\mathbf{x}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{F}_{\mathbf{Y}}^{-1}(\mathbf{Z};\bm{\theta}_{Y}(\mathbf{x}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The components of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> are generally dependent due to the underlying dependence structure in the joint distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>. To characterize this dependence and obtain the joint CDF or probability distribution function (PDF) of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the concept of a copula. By Sklar’s Theorem, any multivariate joint CDF, such as the joint CDF of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, can be expressed in terms of its marginal CDFs <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{Y_{i}}(y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{Y_{i}}(y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and a copula function <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathbf{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathbf{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta})=C_{\mathbf{Y}}(F_{Y_{1}}(y_{1};\bm{\theta}_{1}),\dots,F_{Y_{n_{Y}}}(y_{n_{Y}};\bm{\theta}_{n_{Y}}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta})=C_{\mathbf{Y}}(F_{Y_{1}}(y_{1};\bm{\theta}_{1}),\dots,F_{Y_{n_{Y}}}(y_{n_{Y}};\bm{\theta}_{n_{Y}}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Given the definition <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}=F_{Y_{i}}(Y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}=F_{Y_{i}}(Y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, the joint CDF of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is directly the copula function <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathbf{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathbf{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{Z}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=C_{\mathbf{Y}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐙</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{Z}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=C_{\mathbf{Y}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where each <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. To derive the joint PDF of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we differentiate its joint CDF. This gives the copula density of the distribution of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\mathbf{Y}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\mathbf{Y}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathbf{Z}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=c_{\mathbf{Y}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=\frac{\partial^{n_{Y}}C_{\mathbf{Y}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})}{\partial z_{1}\dots\partial z_{n_{Y}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝐙</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msup><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathbf{Z}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=c_{\mathbf{Y}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=\frac{\partial^{n_{Y}}C_{\mathbf{Y}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})}{\partial z_{1}\dots\partial z_{n_{Y}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, using the change-of-variables formula for PDFs, if the joint PDF <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> exists, and each marginal CDF <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{Y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{Y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable and strictly increasing, then the joint PDF of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathbf{Z}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=f_{\mathbf{Y}}(F_{Y_{1}}^{-1}(z_{1};\bm{\theta}_{1}),\dots,F_{Y_{n_{Y}}}^{-1}(z_{n_{Y}};\bm{\theta}_{n_{Y}}))\\
\times\prod_{i=1}^{n_{Y}}\frac{1}{f_{Y_{i}}(F_{Y_{i}}^{-1}(z_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i});\bm{\theta}_{i})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝐙</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">×</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathbf{Z}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=f_{\mathbf{Y}}(F_{Y_{1}}^{-1}(z_{1};\bm{\theta}_{1}),\dots,F_{Y_{n_{Y}}}^{-1}(z_{n_{Y}};\bm{\theta}_{n_{Y}}))\\
\times\prod_{i=1}^{n_{Y}}\frac{1}{f_{Y_{i}}(F_{Y_{i}}^{-1}(z_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i});\bm{\theta}_{i})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.5" class="ltx_p">This shows that the joint PDF of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is the copula density, thereby encapsulating the dependence structure of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> while having uniform marginals.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Transformation of Model Uncertainty Variables (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While each <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly distributed, the components of the vector <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> may still be statistically dependent due to the inherent dependencies among the ML model outputs. However, some numerical algorithms require independent standard normal variables. To obtain such variables from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we perform a two-step transformation:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 1: Transform Uniform Variables (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>) to Correlated Standard Normal Variables (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each component <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is transformed into a standard normal variable <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by applying the inverse standard normal CDF, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}=\Phi^{-1}(Z_{i})\quad\text{for }i=1,\dots,n_{Y}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}=\Phi^{-1}(Z_{i})\quad\text{for }i=1,\dots,n_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The resulting vector <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}=(W_{1},\dots,W_{n_{Y}})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}=(W_{1},\dots,W_{n_{Y}})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of standard normal variables. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> is a correlated standard normal vector, preserving the dependence structure of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> (and thus the dependence of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>). Its joint distribution is a multivariate normal distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{\Sigma_{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{\Sigma_{W}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma_{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma_{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the correlation matrix of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>. The elements of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma_{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma_{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the correlation coefficients between <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The correlation matrix <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma_{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma_{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> is generally not analytically available for arbitrary non-Gaussian distributions <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In such cases, it must be estimated numerically. A common approach involves generating a large number of samples of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> (using Monte Carlo simulation based on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta}_{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta}_{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>), transforming these <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> samples to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> samples using (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.2 Case 1: No Input Uncertainty in 𝐗 (X=x, deterministic) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ System-Level Uncertainty Quantification with Multiple Machine Learning Models: A Theoretical Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), then to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> samples using (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In Step 1: Transform Uniform Variables (𝐙) to Correlated Standard Normal Variables (𝐖) ‣ 3.2.1 Transformation of Model Uncertainty Variables (𝐙 to 𝐔_𝑍) ‣ 3.2 Case 1: No Input Uncertainty in 𝐗 (X=x, deterministic) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ System-Level Uncertainty Quantification with Multiple Machine Learning Models: A Theoretical Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), and finally computing the sample correlation matrix of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma_{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma_{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 2: Decorrelate Correlated Standard Normals (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>) to Independent Standard Normals (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain a vector of independent standard normal variables from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply a whitening transformation using the Cholesky decomposition of its correlation matrix <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma_{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma_{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{W}</annotation></semantics></math> be the lower triangular Cholesky factor of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma_{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma_{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma_{W}}=C_{W}C_{W}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma_{W}}=C_{W}C_{W}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The vector of independent standard Gaussian variables <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{Z}=(U_{Z,1},\dots,U_{Z,n_{Y}})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{Z}=(U_{Z,1},\dots,U_{Z,n_{Y}})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is then obtained by:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\mathbf{U}_{Z}=C_{W}^{-1}\mathbf{W}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐖</mi></mrow></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\mathbf{U}_{Z}=C_{W}^{-1}\mathbf{W}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Each component <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{Z,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{Z,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is now an independent standard normal random variable. This transformation can be generally denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{Z}=T_{Z}(\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{Z}=T_{Z}(\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, and its inverse as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}=T_{Z}^{-1}(\mathbf{U}_{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}=T_{Z}^{-1}(\mathbf{U}_{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Final Expression for Epistemic Uncertainty</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By performing the transformations described above, we can express the ML model outputs solely as functions of independent standard normal variables. We substitute the expression for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> from the inverse of (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In Step 2: Decorrelate Correlated Standard Normals (𝐖) to Independent Standard Normals (𝐔_𝑍) ‣ 3.2.1 Transformation of Model Uncertainty Variables (𝐙 to 𝐔_𝑍) ‣ 3.2 Case 1: No Input Uncertainty in 𝐗 (X=x, deterministic) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ System-Level Uncertainty Quantification with Multiple Machine Learning Models: A Theoretical Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) (i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}=\Phi(C_{W}\mathbf{U}_{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}=\Phi(C_{W}\mathbf{U}_{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>) into (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2 Case 1: No Input Uncertainty in 𝐗 (X=x, deterministic) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ System-Level Uncertainty Quantification with Multiple Machine Learning Models: A Theoretical Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{F}_{\mathbf{Y}}^{-1}(T_{Z}^{-1}(\mathbf{U}_{Z});\bm{\theta}_{Y})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{F}_{\mathbf{Y}}^{-1}(T_{Z}^{-1}(\mathbf{U}_{Z});\bm{\theta}_{Y})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This can be compactly written as:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\mathbf{Y}=\mathcal{G}_{e}(\mathbf{U}_{Z})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\mathbf{Y}=\mathcal{G}_{e}(\mathbf{U}_{Z})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> is the composite function representing the ML models and the probabilistic transformations when only epistemic uncertainty is considered.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Case 2: General Case with Aleatory Uncertainty in <math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> (X is random)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section outlines the theoretical framework for quantifying system uncertainty in ML model predictions for the general case. The primary task is to determine the joint distribution of multiple ML model outputs, considering both input and model uncertainties, particularly when model uncertainties are statistically dependent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The core strategy of this framework is to transform all sources of uncertainty—both input uncertainties (<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>) and model uncertainties (represented by an auxiliary vector <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>)—into a unified space of independent standard normal variables. This is particularly crucial because the parameters governing the model uncertainty (e.g., means and variances of ML predictions) are often functions of the random inputs <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to a strong coupling between these two types of uncertainty. This decoupling significantly simplifies subsequent uncertainty propagation and analysis.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Transformation of Input Variables (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The input vector <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> (size <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>) can consist of random variables that may be statistically dependent and follow arbitrary marginal distributions. We transform <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> into a vector of independent standard normal variables, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{X}=(U_{X,1},\dots,U_{X,n_{X}})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{X}=(U_{X,1},\dots,U_{X,n_{X}})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. This transformation is a standard procedure in physics-based reliability analysis, often achieved through methods like the Nataf transformation or sequential inverse CDF transformations such as Rosenblatt Transformation. Such a transformation allows us to express <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the transformation function. Each component <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{X,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{X,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is an independent standard normal random variable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Decoupling Model Uncertainty via <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math></h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each ML model output <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the vector <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, its probabilistic response given <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is characterized by a conditional distribution <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}(Y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}(Y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}))</annotation></semantics></math>. The auxiliary scalar random variable <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}=F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}(Y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}))\quad\text{for }i=1,\dots,n_{Y}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}=F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}(Y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}))\quad\text{for }i=1,\dots,n_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">As discussed previously, each <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> follows a uniform distribution on <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}\sim Unif[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}\sim Unif[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>) and is independent of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. The vector <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}=(Z_{1},\dots,Z_{n_{Y}})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}=(Z_{1},\dots,Z_{n_{Y}})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the conditional dependence structure among the model uncertainties of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. The original ML model output vector <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> can then be recovered by inverting this transformation for each component:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}=F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}^{-1}(Z_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}))\quad\text{for }i=1,\dots,n_{Y}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}=F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}^{-1}(Z_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}))\quad\text{for }i=1,\dots,n_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In vector form, this can be written as:</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{F}_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}^{-1}(\mathbf{Z};\bm{\theta}_{Y}(\mathbf{X}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{F}_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}^{-1}(\mathbf{Z};\bm{\theta}_{Y}(\mathbf{X}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">This equation explicitly shows that <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is a function of the random input variables <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and the model uncertainty variables <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are now decoupled. The components of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> are generally dependent due to the underlying dependence structure in the conditional joint distribution <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta}(\mathbf{X}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta}(\mathbf{X}))</annotation></semantics></math>. The multivariate joint CDF of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta}(\mathbf{X}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta}(\mathbf{X}))</annotation></semantics></math>, can be expressed in terms of its conditional marginal CDFs <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}(y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}(y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}))</annotation></semantics></math> and a copula function <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta}(\mathbf{X}))=C_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(F_{Y_{1}|\mathbf{X}}(y_{1};\bm{\theta}_{1}(\mathbf{X})),\dots,F_{Y_{n_{Y}}|\mathbf{X}}(y_{n_{Y}};\bm{\theta}_{n_{Y}}(\mathbf{X})))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta}(\mathbf{X}))=C_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(F_{Y_{1}|\mathbf{X}}(y_{1};\bm{\theta}_{1}(\mathbf{X})),\dots,F_{Y_{n_{Y}}|\mathbf{X}}(y_{n_{Y}};\bm{\theta}_{n_{Y}}(\mathbf{X})))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Given the definition <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}=F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}(Y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}=F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}(Y_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}))</annotation></semantics></math>, the joint CDF of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is directly the copula function <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathbf{Z}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=C_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐙</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathbf{Z}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=C_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">where each <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. To derive the joint PDF of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we differentiate its joint CDF. This gives the copula density of the conditional distribution of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathbf{Z}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=c_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=\frac{\partial^{n_{Y}}C_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})}{\partial z_{1}\dots\partial z_{n_{Y}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝐙</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msup><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathbf{Z}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=c_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=\frac{\partial^{n_{Y}}C_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})}{\partial z_{1}\dots\partial z_{n_{Y}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.7" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, using the change-of-variables formula for PDFs, if the conditional joint PDF <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta}(\mathbf{X}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{y};\bm{\theta}(\mathbf{X}))</annotation></semantics></math> exists, and each conditional marginal CDF <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable and strictly increasing, then the joint PDF of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathbf{Z}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=f_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(F_{Y_{1}|\mathbf{X}}^{-1}(z_{1};\bm{\theta}_{1}(\mathbf{X})),\dots,F_{Y_{n_{Y}}|\mathbf{X}}^{-1}(z_{n_{Y}};\bm{\theta}_{n_{Y}}(\mathbf{X})))\\
\times\prod_{i=1}^{n_{Y}}\frac{1}{f_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}(F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}^{-1}(z_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}));\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}))}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝐙</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">×</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathbf{Z}}(z_{1},\dots,z_{n_{Y}})=f_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(F_{Y_{1}|\mathbf{X}}^{-1}(z_{1};\bm{\theta}_{1}(\mathbf{X})),\dots,F_{Y_{n_{Y}}|\mathbf{X}}^{-1}(z_{n_{Y}};\bm{\theta}_{n_{Y}}(\mathbf{X})))\\
\times\prod_{i=1}^{n_{Y}}\frac{1}{f_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}(F_{Y_{i}|\mathbf{X}}^{-1}(z_{i};\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}));\bm{\theta}_{i}(\mathbf{X}))}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.8" class="ltx_p">This shows that the joint PDF of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is the copula density, thereby encapsulating the conditional dependence structure of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> while having uniform marginals.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Transformation of Model Uncertainty Variables (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While each <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly distributed and independent of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, the components of the vector <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> may still be statistically dependent due to the inherent dependencies among the ML model outputs. To obtain independent standard normal variables from <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we perform the same two-step transformation as described in Sec. 3.2. The final result is</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\mathbf{U}_{Z}=C_{W}(\mathbf{X})^{-1}\mathbf{W}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐖</mi></mrow></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\mathbf{U}_{Z}=C_{W}(\mathbf{X})^{-1}\mathbf{W}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each component <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{Z,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{Z,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is now an independent standard normal random variable. This transformation can be generally denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{Z}=T_{Z}(\mathbf{Z};\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{Z}=T_{Z}(\mathbf{Z};\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, and its inverse as <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}=T_{Z}^{-1}(\mathbf{U}_{Z};\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}=T_{Z}^{-1}(\mathbf{U}_{Z};\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Unified Independent Space and Final Expression</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By performing the transformations described above, we can express all sources of uncertainty in a unified space of independent standard normal variables. We combine the transformed input variables <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and the transformed model uncertainty variables <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> into a single comprehensive vector <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}=(\mathbf{U}_{X},\mathbf{U}_{Z})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}=(\mathbf{U}_{X},\mathbf{U}_{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">All components of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are now independent standard normal random variables. Finally, we substitute the expressions for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> from (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Transformation of Input Variables (𝐗 to 𝐔_𝑋) ‣ 3.3 Case 2: General Case with Aleatory Uncertainty in 𝐗 (X is random) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ System-Level Uncertainty Quantification with Multiple Machine Learning Models: A Theoretical Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> from the inverse of (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In Transformation of Model Uncertainty Variables (𝐙 to 𝐔_𝑍) ‣ 3.3 Case 2: General Case with Aleatory Uncertainty in 𝐗 (X is random) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ System-Level Uncertainty Quantification with Multiple Machine Learning Models: A Theoretical Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) (i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}=\Phi(C_{W}(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{U}_{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}=\Phi(C_{W}(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{U}_{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>) into (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Decoupling Model Uncertainty via 𝐙 ‣ 3.3 Case 2: General Case with Aleatory Uncertainty in 𝐗 (X is random) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ System-Level Uncertainty Quantification with Multiple Machine Learning Models: A Theoretical Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{F}_{\mathbf{Y}|T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X})}^{-1}(T_{Z}^{-1}(\mathbf{U}_{Z};T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X}));\bm{\theta}_{Y}(T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X})))}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{F}_{\mathbf{Y}|T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X})}^{-1}(T_{Z}^{-1}(\mathbf{U}_{Z};T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X}));\bm{\theta}_{Y}(T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X})))}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This can be compactly written as:</p>
<table id="S3.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\mathbf{Y}=\mathcal{G}(\mathbf{U}_{X},\mathbf{U}_{Z})=\mathcal{G}(\mathbf{U})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\mathbf{Y}=\mathcal{G}(\mathbf{U}_{X},\mathbf{U}_{Z})=\mathcal{G}(\mathbf{U})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is the composite function representing the ML models and all the probabilistic transformations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Advantages of Transformation to Independent Standard Normal Variables</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This transformation offers significant advantages for UQ:</p>
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decoupling of Uncertainties:</span> The primary benefit is the explicit decoupling of input uncertainty and model uncertainty. This allows for a clear separation and independent treatment of aleatory and epistemic uncertainties, even when they are originally coupled.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simplified Uncertainty Propagation:</span> By expressing <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of only independent standard normal variables <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, subsequent uncertainty propagation (e.g., moment methods, fast probability integration, or other advanced UQ techniques) becomes significantly simpler and more computationally efficient. Many UQ algorithms are optimized for such inputs; for example, the intergrand for the fast probability integration becomes symmetric multivariate standard normal PDF.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Facilitated Sensitivity Analysis:</span> This framework naturally lends itself to sensitivity analysis. Techniques like Sobol’ indices, which require independent input variables, can be directly applied to <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}(\mathbf{U})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}(\mathbf{U})</annotation></semantics></math> to quantify the contribution of each independent uncertainty source (i.e., each component of <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>) to the total variance of <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. This provides valuable insights into the most influential sources of uncertainty.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>GP Models</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we apply the framework developed in Sec. 3.3 to GP models. For a multi-output GP model, the prediction at a new input <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> (test point) is a vector of random variables, <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}=(Y_{1},\dots,Y_{n_{Y}})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}=(Y_{1},\dots,Y_{n_{Y}})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose joint distribution is a multivariate Gaussian. This is expressed as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mu}(\mathbf{X}),\bm{\Sigma}(\mathbf{X}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mu}(\mathbf{X}),\bm{\Sigma}(\mathbf{X}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is the vector of predictive means and <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is the predictive covariance matrix, both of which are functions of the input vector <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. The parameters of this conditional distribution are given by <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}_{Y}(\mathbf{X})=\{\bm{\mu}(\mathbf{X}),\bm{\Sigma}(\mathbf{X})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}_{Y}(\mathbf{X})=\{\bm{\mu}(\mathbf{X}),\bm{\Sigma}(\mathbf{X})\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following the methodology in Sec. 3.3, we can derive the necessary equations for the GP case.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Transformation to Independent Standard Normal Variables</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The core of the methodology is to transform the dependent input random variables into a set of independent standard normal variables.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1" class="ltx_subparagraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subparagraph">Step 1: Transform Correlated Gaussian Variables (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>) to Independent Standard Normals (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h6>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given that <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is a multivariate normal vector conditional on <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, the transformation to independent standard normal variables is straightforward and analytical. We perform a whitening transformation using the Cholesky decomposition of the conditional covariance matrix <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{Y}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{Y}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> be the lower triangular Cholesky factor of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}(\mathbf{x})=C_{Y}(\mathbf{x})C_{Y}(\mathbf{x})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}(\mathbf{x})=C_{Y}(\mathbf{x})C_{Y}(\mathbf{x})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The vector of independent standard Gaussian variables <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{Y}=(U_{Y,1},\dots,U_{Y,n_{Y}})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{Y}=(U_{Y,1},\dots,U_{Y,n_{Y}})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is then obtained by:</p>
<table id="S4.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{Y}=C_{Y}(\mathbf{X})^{-1}(\mathbf{Y}-\bm{\mu}(\mathbf{x}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{Y}=C_{Y}(\mathbf{X})^{-1}(\mathbf{Y}-\bm{\mu}(\mathbf{x}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Each component <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{Y,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{Y,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is now an independent standard normal random variable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx2" class="ltx_subparagraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subparagraph">Step 2: Express <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> as a Function of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math></h6>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can express the original ML prediction vector <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of the independent standard normal variables <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> by inverting the transformation in (<a href="#S4.E24" title="In Step 1: Transform Correlated Gaussian Variables (𝐘) to Independent Standard Normals (𝐔_𝑌) ‣ Transformation to Independent Standard Normal Variables ‣ 4 GP Models ‣ System-Level Uncertainty Quantification with Multiple Machine Learning Models: A Theoretical Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S4.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\mathbf{Y}=\bm{\mu}(\mathbf{X})+C_{Y}(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{U}_{Y}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\mathbf{Y}=\bm{\mu}(\mathbf{X})+C_{Y}(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{U}_{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This equation is analogous to (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2.2 Final Expression for Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ 3.2 Case 1: No Input Uncertainty in 𝐗 (X=x, deterministic) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ System-Level Uncertainty Quantification with Multiple Machine Learning Models: A Theoretical Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), where the transformations <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.SPx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Z}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Z}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> simplify significantly due to the Gaussian nature of the GP predictions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Unified Independent Space</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the general case with random inputs <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the same strategy as in Sec. 3.3. We have the transformation for the input variables:</p>
<table id="S4.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the relationship for the GP outputs derived above, which is now a function of the random input <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}=\bm{\mu}(\mathbf{X})+C_{Y}(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{U}_{Y}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}=\bm{\mu}(\mathbf{X})+C_{Y}(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{U}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We define the total vector of independent standard normal variables as <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}=(\mathbf{U}_{X},\mathbf{U}_{Y})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}=(\mathbf{U}_{X},\mathbf{U}_{Y})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting the expression for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> into the equation for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get the final form analogous to (<a href="#S3.E23" title="In 3.3.1 Unified Independent Space and Final Expression ‣ 3.3 Case 2: General Case with Aleatory Uncertainty in 𝐗 (X is random) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ System-Level Uncertainty Quantification with Multiple Machine Learning Models: A Theoretical Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S4.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\mathbf{Y}=\bm{\mu}(T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X}))+C_{Y}(T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X}))\mathbf{U}_{Y}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\mathbf{Y}=\bm{\mu}(T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X}))+C_{Y}(T_{X}(\mathbf{U}_{X}))\mathbf{U}_{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">This can be written compactly as <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}=\mathcal{G}(\mathbf{U})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}=\mathcal{G}(\mathbf{U})</annotation></semantics></math>, where all components of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent standard normal random variables. This result simplifies the general framework for GP models because the probability integral transform and its inverse steps (represented by <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Z}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Z}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the general case) are naturally replaced by the simpler Cholesky transformation due to the Gaussian assumption.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Numerical Algorithm Development</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Numerical algorithms can be developed based on the proposed theoretical framework. Accurate numerical algorithms are essential for system UQ. Without it, the benefit of accounting for ML model uncertainty is greatly diminished. Challenges must be addressed for the development.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The primary issue is that existing fast probability integration methods, while effective for some problems, can introduce substantial errors. This is particularly true when applied to the expanded uncertainty space that includes model uncertainty. These errors are caused by two main factors. First, the integration boundaries, determined by equations such as (15), (23), and (28), may be highly irregular, leading to numerical instability. This is caused by model uncertainty that varies in the space of random input variables. Second, the ML model’s nonlinearity, when coupled with this irregular boundary, can result in large integration errors, which compromise the benefits of propagating model uncertainty. To overcome these challenges, it is important to carefully and deliberately treat the model uncertainty variable to reduce its impact and improve the accuracy of the numerical integration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Handling high-dimensional dependent model outputs in system UQ is a nontrivial task. Numerical methods for integrating high-dimensional joint PDF are currently limited, especially when dependencies exist among model responses. For example, computing the joint probability or CDF of a multivariate normal distribution typically relies on sampling-based methods when the dimensionality exceeds three. These methods often lack accuracy in estimating rare failure events due to the curse of dimensionality and insufficient sampling in critical regions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. As a result, efficient and accurate numerical integration techniques are urgently needed for problems involving a large number of statistically dependent outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, the Monte Carlo method can be used directly with decoupled uncertainty. Although it is straightforward to use the MC method, it is inefficient for rare joint probabilities in the range between <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-9}</annotation></semantics></math>. The Monte Carlo method may also cause numerical problems when used in optimization, such as reliability-based design optimization. Therefore, numerical methods are preferred over sampling methods for practical engineering applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusions</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents a theoretical framework that provides essential formulations for developing numerical algorithms to quantify system uncertainty. The core of this framework is its ability to decouple two distinct types of uncertainty: the randomness in input variables and the inherent uncertainty of machine learning models. This decoupling is achieved through specific formulations that expand the uncertainty space and establish new formulations, as defined by equations such as (15), (23), and (28). These foundational steps are critical for accurate uncertainty quantification that accounts for the complete range of system-level variables. By providing these key formulations, our framework directly supports the development of new numerical algorithms tailored to address the challenges of model uncertainty. It highlights that the successful implementation of such algorithms relies on a deliberate approach to manage the irregular integration boundaries and the nonlinearity of ML models, which are often the primary sources of numerical instability. This work therefore serves as a vital blueprint for future research aimed at creating efficient and reliable numerical methods that are essential for practical engineering applications, especially those that involve rare joint probabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
